SUMMARY:PhD Brown Bag Seminar – Ray Gomez (University of Melbourne) – A
  Decision-Focused Learning Approach to Poverty Targeting
LOCATION:Room 304\, FBE Building\, 111 Barry St\, Carlton VIC 3053
DESCRIPTION:Ray Gomez: “A Decision-Focused Learning Approach to Poverty T
 argeting”\n\nGoogle Maps: https://maps.app.goo.gl/BirLkJKurX9pSbBB8\n\nA
 BSTRACT:\nWe propose a decision-focused learning (DFL) approach to poverty
  targeting. We consider the setting where a government observes household 
 covariates but not true consumption\, and must decide whom to target for a
  uniform cash transfer financed from a fixed budget. Conventional targetin
 g methods determine eligibility using a linear predictive model (i.e.\, 
 “proxy means tests”) trained to minimize a statistical loss function s
 uch as mean squared error. The resulting predictions are then used to rank
  or classify households\, but the prediction objective is disconnected fro
 m the policy goal of reducing poverty. Our DFL approach bypasses this two-
 stage procedure by directly minimizing post-transfer poverty outcomes\, su
 ch as the poverty headcount or aggregate poverty gap\, under the budget co
 nstraint. We adopt a tree-based method that learns interpretable targeting
  rules from covariates and simulate theoretical properties of the estimato
 r using Monte Carlo studies. Using living standards survey data from Burki
 na Faso\, our model outperforms both the baseline linear proxy means test 
 and comparable tree- and forest-based machine learning methods in reducing
  post-transfer poverty.
END:VEVENT
