SUMMARY:PhD Brown Bag Seminar – Sonia Ma (University of Melbourne) – Ge
 nder norms and Confucian legacies in\nRepublican China
LOCATION:Room 304\, FBE Building\, 111 Barry St\, Carlton VIC 3053
DESCRIPTION:Sonia Ma: “Gender norms and Confucian legacies in\nRepublican
  China”\n\nGoogle Maps: https://maps.app.goo.gl/BirLkJKurX9pSbBB8\n\nABS
 TRACT:\nHow do traditional cultural norms shape women’s roles during soc
 ial and political transition? We examine this question in Republican China
  (1911-1949)\, a time of ideological conflict between Confucian values and
  emerging Western ideals of democracy\, science\, and women’s emancipati
 on. We study the relationship between traditional gender norms and three d
 imensions of women’s emancipation using newly compiled county-level data
 : tertiary education enrolment\, recognition as members of the social elit
 e\, and participation in the early communist movement. We construct a nove
 l county-level index of Confucian influence based on the prevalence of the
  term lienü (chaste women) in local histories—women celebrated posthumo
 usly for their virtue in accordance with Confucian moral codes. To address
  endogeneity concerns\, we employ an instrumental variables approach using
  proximity to the birthplaces of two Confucian female sages. Our 2SLS esti
 mates show that stronger Confucian influence discouraged women’s educati
 on and political activism\, but was associated with a greater overall pres
 ence of notable women in elite roles. Our study contributes new data and a
  novel approach to understanding cultural persistence amid societal change
 .
END:VEVENT
